# frozen_string_literal: true

# Kiosk configuration for this application.
#
# See https://kiosk.tech and the kiosk-server README for the full option
# surface. Defaults here reflect the values the install generator was
# invoked with — uncomment and adjust as needed.

Kiosk.configure do |c|
  # ─── Principal (user) wiring ─────────────────────────────────────────
  # Provider's user model class name as a String — resolved at request
  # time by kiosk-server, not eagerly, to avoid load-order issues.
  c.user_model     = "<%= options[:user_table].classify %>"

  # Type of users.id — :uuid (default), :bigint, :integer, or :text.
  c.user_id_type   = :<%= options[:user_id_type] %>

  # Column on `users` that holds the principal id. Default :id.
  c.user_id_column = :id

  # ─── Handler controllers ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The controllers that declare this origin's wire verbs — classes that
  # `include Kiosk::Handler`. NAME them here; the
  # engine loads and registers them once at boot and again after every
  # reload in development.
  #
  # Empty because this generator runs before you have written any. Leave it
  # empty and this origin serves NO verbs: a handler registers when its
  # class body is read, nothing in your app ever references one on its own
  # (the wire reaches it THROUGH the registry), so GET <mount>/schema
  # answers with an empty catalog, every verb path answers 404, and
  # /.well-known/kiosk.json advertises "capabilities": []. Fill this in as
  # you add handler controllers — see "Declaring queries and actions" in
  # the kiosk-server README, e.g.:
  #   c.handlers = %w[Kiosk::CatalogController Kiosk::OrdersController]
  c.handlers = []

  # ─── Validation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # WHAT IS NOT A SETTING, so you know what these two flags are NOT for:
  # coerce-then-validate on the per-verb wire is UNCONDITIONAL. Every call to
  # <mount>/<verb-name> has its arguments coerced and checked against that
  # verb's declared `input_schema` before your handler sees one, whatever is
  # written here — spec §8.1 item 5 makes that an operator obligation, so a
  # flag that could switch it off would be a flag for serving a non-conformant
  # origin. It is why `json_schemer` is a real dependency of kiosk-server
  # rather than an optional extra. The two flags below are different, smaller
  # things, and both are opt-in with a default of `false`.

  # The PoW-SHAPE check in front of the proof-of-work gate. A `Kiosk-PoW`
  # header whose proofs cannot be parsed otherwise yields a fresh 402
  # challenge, forever, with no diagnostic — the caller retries into a silent
  # loop and nothing in your logs says why. On, a malformed shape is a clear
  # 400 naming the shape it expected. It is NOT the gate: a well-formed but
  # forged proof still fails the real cryptographic check. An absent proof is
  # untouched, so the ordinary first no-pow request still gets its 402.
  c.validate_requests = true

  # Every query/action ANSWER validated against the `output_schema` that verb
  # declares, so a descriptor that lies about its handler fails loudly instead
  # of shipping a wrong shape to an assistant.
  #
  # NOT `true`, and the reason is the one kiosk-server's own
  # response_validation.rb gives: with it on in production a descriptor typo
  # becomes a 500 for a caller who did nothing wrong. Nothing a caller sends
  # can trigger this check — the failure it reports is always yours — so it
  # belongs in development and CI, where it is cheap to fix, and not in front
  # of a live assistant. Your test suite keeps the whole assertion.
  c.validate_responses = !Rails.env.production?

  # ─── Postgres surface ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # GUC namespace used in SET LOCAL statements (and RLS policies, if you
  # opt in). Default "app" — change if your backend already uses app.*.
  # c.guc_namespace = "<%= options[:guc_namespace] %>"

  # Postgres schema for Kiosk helper functions and tables.
  # c.schema = "<%= options[:schema] %>"

  # ─── Opt-in: DB-level RLS defense-in-depth ───────────────────────────
  # Kiosk's isolation comes from the sanctioned per-verb surface with
  # app-layer authz. Postgres RLS is available as belt-and-suspenders:
  # add `gem "kiosk-rls"`, include Kiosk::RLS::DSL into your migrations,
  # and configure the DB roles (provider creates these — Kiosk does not).
  # c.app_role        = "app_role"      # runtime role with NOBYPASSRLS
  # c.system_role     = "system_role"   # privileged role; owns the tables
  # c.enforce_db_role = true            # SET LOCAL ROLE app_role per request

  # ─── Wire surface ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Issuer URL — MUST match `kiosk.issuer` in /.well-known/kiosk.json AND
  # be the exact origin assistants dial (scheme + host, no trailing slash).
  # It is configured, not derived from the request Host, because it anchors
  # both the AP2 mandate `iss` and the `aud` origin-binding that Kiosk checks
  # by strict equality — derived from the request it would defend nothing.
  # Get it wrong and the app still boots, then rejects EVERY assistant with
  # "proof audience mismatch". One instance therefore serves exactly one
  # origin: point aliases at it with a redirect before any Kiosk verb.
  # c.issuer = "https://<your-host>"

  # Role vocabulary your authz (app-layer policies, and RLS if opted in)
  # branches on. Never include `:admin` — prefer job-titled roles like
  # `:support`, `:master`.
  #
  # ROLES ARE TOTAL, OR YOU HAVE NONE. If you declare roles here, your
  # `user_idp` MUST resolve one for EVERY human who can approve an assistant
  # binding. A role for staff and nothing for customers is NOT a supported
  # configuration: on an origin with more than one role, a binding that
  # resolves no role leaves the assistant's existing role in place, so an
  # assistant already holding the privileged role KEEPS it while its principal
  # changes to a human who holds none. Declaring no roles at all is the other
  # supported shape — then no binding carries a role and no token has one.
  # Both are total; the MIXTURE is what is unsupported (kiosk.tech
  # `protocol.md` §6.3).
  #
  # In practice the only way to acquire the mixture is to write `#kiosk_role`
  # on your user model and let it answer nil. Make it TOTAL: return your
  # least-privileged declared role instead. Nothing can check this at boot —
  # whether a method is total over your humans is not a fact a configuration
  # file holds — so kiosk-server warns in your log the first time a ceremony
  # resolves no role on a multi-role origin. That warning is the only signal
  # you get, and it arrives at the first binding rather than at deploy.
  # c.roles = %i[customer]

  # Role pinned to every self-registered agent (agents cannot choose their
  # own). OPTIONAL: when unset, self-registered agents get no role — assign
  # roles inside the `c.assistant_creation` hook instead if your app
  # branches on them. When set, must be one of `c.roles`.
  # c.registration_role = :customer

  # Owner block surfaced in /.well-known/kiosk.json.
  # c.owner = { name: "Your Company", support: "support@example.com" }

  # ─── Pluggable identity adapters ─────────────────────────────────────
  # Two DIFFERENT, both-OPTIONAL seams. You need NEITHER to start:
  #
  # AGENT-IdP (c.agent_idp) — verifies/mints AI-assistant credentials. Defaults
  # to the bundled kiosk-pop DefaultAgentIdp, used with ZERO config: it verifies
  # the tokens the register/login endpoints mint. Set it ONLY to front an
  # external agent-identity issuer by implementing
  # `Kiosk::AgentIdentityProviders::Base` — the only subclass Kiosk ships is
  # the bundled DefaultAgentIdp named above, so an adapter fronting an
  # external issuer is yours to write. If you do write one: the `agent_id` your
  # adapter puts in a `Kiosk::Identity` MUST be a UUID string. Every agent_id
  # column these migrations create — and the `current_agent_id()` helper RLS
  # policies call — is typed `uuid`, with no equivalent of `user_id_type` to
  # widen it, so an opaque external agent identifier has to be mapped onto a
  # local uuid inside the adapter.
  #   # c.agent_idp = MyExternalAgentIdp.new   # your Kiosk::AgentIdentityProviders::Base subclass
  #
  # USER-IdP (c.user_idp) — binds a real HUMAN account (and optionally its role)
  # from your EXISTING session auth, so a human can approve/manage assistant
  # links from your own frontend. The shipped worked example is
  # `kiosk-user-idp-devise` (add `gem "kiosk-user-idp-devise"`):
  #   # c.user_idp = Kiosk::UserIdentityProviders::Devise.new

  # ─── If you run MORE THAN ONE process, read this ─────────────────────
  # The PoW gate proves a challenge is single-use by remembering spent
  # challenge ids, and the DEFAULT memory for that is an in-process Hash.
  # That is correct for exactly one process and for nothing else. Two Puma
  # workers, two dynos, two pods — or a rolling deploy where the old and the
  # new process overlap for a minute — each keep their OWN memory, so one
  # proof is accepted once PER PROCESS and a toll you priced at N proofs is
  # really worth N times the number of processes.
  #
  # WHY THIS IS SPELLED OUT HERE INSTEAD OF LEFT FOR YOU TO NOTICE: you
  # cannot notice it. A replayed proof is not an error — it verifies, it is
  # accepted, the request succeeds. There is no exception, no metric, no log
  # line, no failed request, nothing in any dashboard. An operator who scales
  # from one worker to two gets no signal at all that their origin stopped
  # conforming (kiosk.tech `protocol.md` §15.2 and the §16.1 operator
  # profile), which is why it is written down rather than detected.
  #
  # The fix is one table plus one line — both in the kiosk-server README
  # under "Multi-process deployments". The table is deliberately NOT part of
  # `bin/rails g kiosk:install`, because a single-process operator does not
  # need it:
  #   # c.pow_spent_store = Kiosk::Server::PowSpentStores::ActiveRecord.new
  #
  # `c.auth_challenge_store` is in-process for the same reason, but it fails
  # the OTHER way: a challenge issued by one process is invisible to the one
  # that gets the register/login, so the handshake breaks LOUDLY and you will
  # find that one without being told.

  # ─── The event tail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # WHERE EVENTS YOU EMIT ARE KEPT UNTIL THE SUBSCRIBER READS THEM. Unlike
  # the two stores above, this one IS laid down by `bin/rails g kiosk:install`
  # (migration 007) and IS set here, because the in-process default is wrong
  # for every deployment rather than only for a multi-process one.
  #
  # The reason is a property of the protocol and not of your server. Some
  # topics are WAITS — the assistant asked for something and is holding on for
  # the answer, seconds or minutes. Others are SUBSCRIPTIONS: a delivery event
  # arrives hours after the order, a shared-list event whenever somebody else
  # gets round to it. NOTHING holds a socket that long. An assistant is
  # turn-based; it has no process that outlives its session. So for those
  # topics the assistant reconnects later and asks for everything after the
  # id it last saw — and a tail that was in memory is gone, so the only honest
  # answer is `truncated: true`, which tells it to re-read state through an
  # ordinary verb. That is the rare degraded case; with an in-process store it
  # becomes the answer every time you deploy.
  c.event_store = Kiosk::Server::EventStores::ActiveRecord.new

  # ─── Opt-in: the audit sink ──────────────────────────────────────────
  # Kiosk does NOT keep an audit trail. It OFFERS one: set a callable here
  # and it receives one `Kiosk::Server::ActionEvent` per action invocation —
  # success and failure alike — with the action name, the {user_id, agent_id}
  # pair, the role, the actor, the outcome, the error class and message, the
  # timestamp, and the ARGUMENTS. Unset (the default), nothing is emitted and
  # Kiosk stores nothing anywhere.
  #
  # THE ARGUMENTS ARRIVE IN FULL — whatever your verb takes: an address, a
  # name, a cart, a booking reference. Kiosk does not redact them for you,
  # because the retention policy for your customers' data is yours to set,
  # not Kiosk's to assume. Whatever you write, and wherever you write it,
  # YOU are the data controller for it: retention, encryption at rest,
  # access control and deletion requests all land on you.
  #
  # If you want the shape without the values, that is one call:
  #   # c.audit_sink = ->(e) { Rails.logger.info(e.with_arg_types.to_h.to_json) }
  # Everything, values included — your call, made explicitly:
  #   # c.audit_sink = ->(e) { AuditRow.create!(**e.to_h) }
  # Per-field, because only you know which of your fields are hot:
  #   # c.audit_sink = ->(e) { Siem.record(e.to_h.merge(args: e.args.except(:card_token))) }
  #
  # A sink that raises does not fail the action — the error is logged and the
  # invocation stands.
end
